y were doing  pretty well so i think the people  especially who have  taken that to heart and have been  careful are going to be the ones that  will  you know we've we've conditioned  ourselves to be afraid of that thing  so it's almost like a conditioned  response like a conditioned fear  that now we have to start undoing i've  told my audience that all along i've  followed cdc guidelines even when i  didn't really think i needed to be  wearing a mask on the bike path outdoors  i did it because people around me were  and it was sort of like a social  calculation that that i made  and now as masks are coming off i've  said  you know i was just traveling and where  i went  people weren't wearing masks in grocery  stores so i didn't either but then  when i got back i stick my head into the  grocery store i mostly see masks i put  my mask on  i have no medical reason why i'm doing  that right the cdc guidelines are i  don't need that mask  is it in some way bad that i'm doing  this  merely to just not stand out as the guy  not wearing a mask is there some  some concerning longer term impact of me  behaving in that way  well let's start by understanding why  you behave that way which is because  your brain is wired to behave  that way okay right so if we think about  you know how do children  learn language how do children learn  body like you know  body movements and if we think about as  our language evolves over time you know  we start to use new slang  it's because our brain is literally  wired we're communal  animals as homo sapiens and we look at  what the  other homo sapiens are doing and we are  going to feel like doing the same thing  it's why peer pressure is a thing it's  why fashion fads are a thing it's just  the way that  we're wired now what impact that's gonna  have on you i mean i don't really know i  think it's just part of the way we all  are and and you're gonna kind of be like  monkey see monkey do yeah which to a  sense is fine  i think the real ch